    ],
    "outline": [
      "POLITICAL FRAMEWORK: Kuru dynasty as a fragile confederation. Hastinapura's legitimacy rests on lineage, not conquest. Bhishma's celibacy oath (narrative, high confidence) creates a succession vacuum: he renounces his own claim, making the throne a prize for his nephews' line. This structural flaw—no clear succession rule—is the root political cause of the war.",
      "SUCCESSION DISPUTE: Dhritarashtra (eldest, blind) is passed over for Pandu (younger, able-bodied). Pandu's abdication after hunting accident (narrative, medium confidence) returns rule to Dhritarashtra as regent, not king. Pandu's sons are born posthumously, creating a legitimacy question: are they heirs of Pandu's line or Dhritarashtra's wards? Dhritarashtra's partiality to Duryodhana (narrative, high confidence) converts a legal ambiguity into a factional struggle.",
      "LAKSHAGRIHA (House of Lac): Duryodhana's plot to burn Pandavas (narrative, high confidence). Political mechanism: elimination of rival claimants without open war. Failure reveals Duryodhana's willingness to use covert violence, hardening Pandava distrust. This is the first escalation from court intrigue to existential threat.",
      "MARRIAGE ALLIANCES: (1) Gandhari-Kuru: secures Gandhara's neutrality, but Shakuni's presence at court becomes a channel for anti-Pandava counsel (narrative, high confidence; political interpretation: Shakuni as foreign agent exploiting succession crisis). (2) Draupadi-Pandavas: Panchala's alliance with Pandavas against Hastinapura's dominance; polyandry (narrative, high confidence) is a political consolidation, not just a boon—it binds five brothers to one powerful father-in-law (Drupada). (3) Subhadra-Krishna's sister to Arjuna: Vrishni alliance, giving Pandavas a powerful but non-committal backer. (4) Abhimanyu-Uttara: post-exile marriage to Virata's daughter, securing Matsya's support for the war.",
      "RAJASUYA: Yudhishthira's imperial sacrifice (narrative, high confidence). Political mechanism: claiming suzerainty over other kings. Duryodhana's humiliation at Indraprastha (narrative, medium confidence) is a personal grievance but also a political signal: Hastinapura's primacy is being challenged. This is the proximate cause of the dice game.",
      "DICE GAME: Shakuni's loaded dice (narrative, high confidence). Political mechanism: legalized dispossession. Yudhishthira stakes kingdom, brothers, self, and Draupadi—each wager escalates because he cannot refuse without losing honor. Draupadi's question (was Yudhishthira free when he staked her?) exposes the legal flaw: a man who has lost himself cannot stake others. Bhishma's silence (narrative, high confidence) is politically decisive: the patriarch refuses to rule, preserving his oath but enabling injustice. Dhritarashtra's restoration of losses is a political miscalculation—it acknowledges the game's legitimacy while trying to undo its results, satisfying neither side.",
      "EXILE: 12 years forest + 1 year incognito (narrative, high confidence). Political function: time for both sides to build alliances. Pandavas use it to court allies (Drupada, Virata, Krishna). Kauravas use it to consolidate Hastinapura's resources. Virata year: Arjuna's service as Brihannala (narrative, high confidence) is a political act—it demonstrates Pandava military capability while hidden, and the cattle raid (Kichaka's death, Uttara's rescue) forces Kaurava recognition of Pandava survival.",
      "PEACE MISSION: Krishna's embassy (narrative, high confidence). Political mechanism: last-ditch diplomacy. Krishna offers five villages (narrative, medium confidence; recensions vary) as a face-saving compromise. Duryodhana's refusal ('not even land the size of a needle's point') is a political declaration: he will not share power. Krishna's failure legitimizes the war as a last resort, shifting moral blame to Duryodhana while Pandavas prepare for conflict.",
      "SUPPORTING PLAYERS: Bhishma—oath-bound to serve the throne, not the person; his neutrality during dice game and war command (narrative, high confidence) is a structural constraint, not cowardice. Drona—gratitude-bound to Duryodhana's patronage; his loyalty is transactional, and his death (via Krishna's stratagem) shows how political debts can be exploited. Karna—status-driven; his loyalty to Duryodhana stems from friendship and the promise of kingship (Anga), not kinship; his refusal to switch sides is a political choice, not just loyalty. Shakuni—the strategist; his role is to weaponize Dhritarashtra's partiality. Krishna—the realpolitik; his counsel (e.g., urging Arjuna to fight, using stratagems like Bhishma's death via Shikhandi) prioritizes outcome over chivalry. Draupadi—the political catalyst; her humiliation is a grievance that unifies Pandavas and legitimizes revenge. Vidura—the conscience; his counsel is rejected because it threatens Duryodhana's claim. Dhritarashtra—the enabler; his weakness is not blindness but indecision. Gandhari—the symbolic protest; her blindfold (narrative, high confidence) is a political statement of solidarity with her husband, but it also removes her from decision-making.",
      "WARTIME DECISIONS: (1) Shalya as Karna's charioteer: Duryodhana's political move to demoralize Karna (narrative, high confidence) by making him serve under a king who resents him—a calculated insult that weakens Kaurava command. (2) Bhishma's fall: Krishna's stratagem using Shikhandi (narrative, high confidence) exploits Bhishma's oath not to fight a woman-born warrior—a political manipulation of a personal vow. (3) Drona's death: Krishna's false report of Ashwatthama's death (narrative, high confidence) uses Drona's paternal love as a weapon—showing that political ends justify tactical deception. (4) Karna's death: Krishna's counsel to Arjuna to kill Karna when his chariot wheel is stuck (narrative, high confidence) prioritizes victory over dharma, reflecting the political reality that the war is about power, not honor. (5) Duryodhana's death: his final battle with Bhima (narrative, high confidence) is a personal duel that mirrors the political struggle—legitimacy vs. usurpation.",
      "POLITICAL ECONOMY (INFERENTIAL): Hastinapura's resource base is the central Kuru territory with tributary kings; Indraprastha is a new capital built from conquered land (Khandava forest, narrative, high confidence). The epic provides no fiscal data, so the analysis is inferential: Indraprastha's wealth (from Rajasuya tributes) likely exceeded Hastinapura's, creating a resource imbalance that Duryodhana feared. Military mobilization: both sides rely on vassal kings (e.g., Shalya for Kauravas, Virata for Pandavas), making the war a coalition conflict, not a dynastic duel. The war's scale (18 akshauhinis, narrative, medium confidence) suggests a massive mobilization that strained both kingdoms' resources.",
      "UNCERTAINTY FLAGS: (1) Dice game terms vary across recensions (e.g., whether Draupadi was staked before or after the brothers). (2) Krishna's peace offer of five villages is not in all versions. (3) The number of warriors (18 akshauhinis) is a traditional figure, not a precise count. (4) Political economy analysis is inferential—the epic does not provide fiscal data. (5) All narrative details are from model general knowledge, not observed sources; no external verification is possible in this run."
    ],
